WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.413
- [Instructor] In another video,

00:00:01.413 --> 00:00:03.460
we introduce ourselves to the idea

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.450
of powers of 10.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.100
We saw that if I were to
say 10 to the first power,

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.370
that means that we are just
really going to take one 10.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.340
If we have 10 to the second power,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.850
that means that we're gonna take two 10s,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.600
so a 10 and a 10,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.200
and we're gonna multiply them

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.880
and so that's going to be 100.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.640
If we take 10 to the third power,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.210
that's going to be three
10s multiplied together,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.410
which is equal to 1000,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.760
which is also one
followed by three zeroes.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.580
So you're already
starting to see a pattern.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.890
What we're going to do in this video

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.480
is think about patterns when
we multiply arbitrary things

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.180
or divide arbitrary things

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.660
by powers of 10.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.850
So let's start with a number.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.960
Let's say I will start with 2.3.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.720
And let's first just multiply
by 10 to the first power.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.790
Well, that's the same thing
as just multiplying it by 10,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:07.100
and we've seen already
when you multiply by 10,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.110
you shift all the digits
one place to the left.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.660
So the two, which is the ones place,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.250
one's up in the 10s place,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.020
and then three, which
is in the tenths place

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.990
will end up in the ones place.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.910
So this is just going to be equal to 23.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.360
And it's always good to do a
little bit of a reality check.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.790
If I just had two and if
were to multiply it by 10,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.270
you'd say okay, that's about 20,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.330
so it makes sense that 2.3 times 10 is 23.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.560
But let's keep going.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.880
Now let's multiply 2.3 not
by 10 to the first power,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.000
which is just 10,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.730
but let's multiply it times
10 to the second power.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.150
What is that going to be?

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.933
Pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.450
All right, well 10 to the second power,

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.230
we already know that's equal to 100,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.210
and so when you multiply by 100
or you multiply by 10 twice,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.910
you're just going to shift
every digit two places

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:01.990
to the left.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.470
So let me draw some places here.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.840
So the thing that is in
the ones place will go

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.280
to the hundreds place,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.590
and the thing that is in the tenths place

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.550
will go to the 10s place.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:18.513
And so this two will now be two hundreds.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.450
This 3/10 will now be three 10s

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.820
and we now have zero ones.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.490
And then if we were to
multiply by 10 again,

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.890
so if we were to say 2.3

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.300
times 10 to the third power,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.160
well then we're going to
shift everything three places

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.220
to the left.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.030
10 to the third power.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.963
This is the same thing
as multiplying by 1000.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.320
So 2.3 times 10 to the third,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.410
the two is going to be shifted
three places to the left,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.900
so the two is going to become
2000, which makes sense.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.620
So it's going to be 2000.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.030
The 3/10 is going to shift
two places to the left,

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.970
so it's going to be 300,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.970
and then we now have
zero 10s and zero ones.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.440
So the pattern that you've probably seen

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.430
is if you multiply a number
times 10 to some power,

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:21.050
you are just shifting the digits
to the left by that power.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.120
And if we divide by a power of 10,

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.290
the same thing would happen,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.610
but we would now be
shifting our digits' places

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.600
to the right.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.600
So for example, what is 2.3 divided by,

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.560
divided by 10 to the first power?

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.130
Pause this video and
try to figure that out.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.610
Well 10 to the first power
is the same thing as 10,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.450
so when we divide by 10,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.360
all of our digits are just
going to shift one place

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.193
to the right,

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:49.890
so this two is going to
end up in the tenths place

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.100
and the three is going to end
up in the hundredths place.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.523
So this is going to give us 0.23.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.870
Two is now in the tenths,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.990
three is now in the hundredths,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:02.930
but we could keep going.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.930
What if we were to say 2.3 divided by 10

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.440
to the second power?

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.300
Pause this video, try to
figure out what that is.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.900
Well in this situation,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.570
we are going to shift all the
digits two places to the right

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.020
and so let me put my places here,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.320
so that's the ones,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.910
tenths, hundredths, thousandths.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.630
And so the thing that's
in the ones place is two.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:27.890
It won't just go to the tenths.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.870
It'll go to the hundredths place,

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.280
which you don't quite see here.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.520
It's right over there.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.030
So the two is going to show up here.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.890
And then the three is going to shift

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:39.907
two places to the right,

00:04:39.907 --> 00:04:41.970
and so it's going to end up there,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.160
and we have zero ones

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.260
and we have zero 10s.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.970
So you're probably already
seeing the pattern here again.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:50.320
Whatever the exponent is,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.357
if you're dividing by 10 to that power,

00:04:52.357 --> 00:04:55.120
you're going to shift that power,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.870
the exponent that many times

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:59.790
you're going to shift the
digit that many places

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:00.743
to the right.

